description:
We write:

```json
    "model_usage": {
      "claude-3-7-sonnet-20250219": {
        "input_tokens": 28,
        "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0,
        "cache_read_input_tokens": 0,
        "output_tokens": 22,
        "total_tokens": 50
      }
    }
```

Inspect writes:

```json
    "model_usage": {
      "anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet-latest": {
        "input_tokens": 514,
        "output_tokens": 304,
        "total_tokens": 818,
        "input_tokens_cache_write": 0,
        "input_tokens_cache_read": 0
      }
    }
```

The names are wrong and we only count solver tokens, not scorer ones.

Inspect has a logging mechanism s.t. the names of functions called as well as the arguments supplied are stored in the eval log. e.g.

```json
      "scores": [
        {
          "name": "model_graded_qa",
          "scorer": "model_graded_qa",
          "params": {},
          "metrics": {
            "accuracy": {
              "name": "accuracy",
              "value": 1.0,
              "options": {}
            },
            "stderr": {
              "name": "stderr",
              "value": 0.0,
              "options": {}
            }
          }
        }
      ]
```

This has led to a few one-off solutions in rinspect, e.g.:

https://github.com/simonpcouch/rinspect/blob/4b654dfa188ad7209e165d87ee01f53e13353d98/R/metrics.R#L1-L8

...that support storing this information. These solutions either 1) don't offer an entry point for non-default values or 2) would require a lot of extra work in that case, e.g. along with the return value of a function, returning the name of the function and the arguments supplied to it.

Let's explore how Inspect handles this and consider if there's some sort of wrapper we could apply more generally, e.g. that takes a function and maps it to a function with this sort of "logged" return value, hopefully

1) avoiding too much `substitute()` magic along the way, and
2) effectively applying to user-supplied solvers/scorers/metrics without them knowing
